  "title": "The Neural Correlates of Consciousness",
  "emoji": "💭",
  "color": "#581C87",
  "description": "A short lesson to help you apply The Neural Correlates of Consciousness.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "Flash an image so briefly that a person cannot consciously see it, and their brain still processes it. But something is different.",
      "visual": "📸"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Scientists compare brain activity during conscious and unconscious processing to find the minimal neural signature of awareness.",
      "visual": "📊"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "When a stimulus becomes conscious, it triggers widespread activation across the cortex, especially in prefrontal and parietal.",
      "visual": "🌐"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Unconscious stimuli activate sensory cortex locally but fail to ignite this global broadcast, as if the signal stays trapped.",
      "visual": "🚪"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "The difference between seeing and not seeing may be whether information breaks through from local processing to global brain-wide.",
      "visual": "🔓"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Consciousness might be what happens when information stops being local and starts being.",
      "visual": "📡"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "What are neural correlates of consciousness?",
    "options": [
      "Brain activity patterns associated with conscious experience",
      "The total number of neurons in the brain",
      "Chemical signals that induce sleep"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
